And the first executable Q-lang syntax should be something like:

let name = "Q-lang"
fn greet(name) {
    return "Hello, " + name
}
result = greet(name)
print(result)

Then the AI-native layer can introduce Q-lang primitives:

detect image
analyze object
infer intent
agent "Web4Agent"
run task
verify result

And  orchestration protocol can become first-class:

agent "LMLM"
route task
instruct agent
execute task
verify result
sync context

That is significantly more powerful than treating .q as merely a kdb+/q-compatible syntax. It makes Q-lang the programmable semantic/runtime layer underneath your LMLM + Agent + Web4 architecture.

One issue is already clear: Q_runtime.opy imports q_core and q_parser, but the repository structure we inspected does not yet establish those as a coherent compiler pipeline. That is the first technical gap I would close.

